WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.340
How can a cell answer a signal in about a millisecond?

2
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.480
Most receptors hand their message to something else, such as a G protein or a

3
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.040
kinase. An ion channel hands it to nothing.

4
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:23.460
The signal opens a hole, ions pour through, and the voltage across the membrane

5
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:31.300
changes. One open channel lets about 10 million ions cross every second, so which

6
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.300
way do they go?

7
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.740
Two kinds of protein move ions across a membrane.

8
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.280
A pump binds its ions and changes shape to carry them, uphill if it spends ATP.

9
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.900
A channel is a pore with a gate, which a transmitter, a voltage, or heat can open.

10
00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:57.280
Once it is open, ions run downhill at around 10 million a second, while a pump

11
00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.580
manages a few hundred.

12
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.660
So pumps build the gradients slowly, and channels spend them fast.

13
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:12.423
Downhill has two parts: a push from the difference in concentration, and a pull from

14
00:01:12.423 --> 00:01:14.083
the voltage across the membrane.

15
00:01:15.023 --> 00:01:20.623
Take sodium. A cell keeps it mostly outside, and the inside of a resting cell sits

16
00:01:20.623 --> 00:01:22.523
near −70 millivolts.

17
00:01:23.263 --> 00:01:27.563
For each ion, there is one voltage at which push and pull balance.

18
00:01:28.163 --> 00:01:33.683
That is its equilibrium potential, E, and for sodium it is about +60

19
00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:34.683
millivolts.

20
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:39.733
A cell keeps potassium mostly inside.

21
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:45.853
With typical concentrations, E for potassium is about −90 millivolts.

22
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:51.413
An open channel drags the membrane voltage toward the E of the ion it passes.

23
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:55.393
So the direction of flow follows from one subtraction.

24
00:01:55.993 --> 00:01:59.193
The driving force is the membrane voltage minus E.

25
00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:07.963
At −70 millivolts, sodium is 130 millivolts away from its E. So it rushes in,

26
00:02:08.263 --> 00:02:12.623
and the cell depolarises, meaning its voltage becomes less negative.

27
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:19.203
Potassium is only 20 millivolts from its E. So it leaks out gently, and pulls the

28
00:02:19.203 --> 00:02:20.203
voltage back down.

29
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:26.693
Very few ions need to cross to change the voltage.

30
00:02:26.733 --> 00:02:31.513
So the concentrations barely shift, and nothing has to be made.

31
00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:33.813
The voltage is the message.

32
00:02:34.293 --> 00:02:39.773
So keep this rule: the signal opens a hole, and the gradient does the rest.
